## How the Code Maps to the Strategies

**Synchronous single leader.** `Append` accepts an entry on the leader, sends it to the voters, and commits only when `acks` reaches the quorum of 3. The commit point is the guarantee: any confirmed payment is on at least three nodes, so the election-time rule in `Failover` can always find it. `TestCommittedWritesSurviveFailover` checks that a balance built from committed entries is intact on the new leader.

**Election restriction and fencing.** `Failover` refuses a candidate whose log ends before the commit index, which is the manual form of the election restriction. It then fences the new epoch on a quorum before switching leaders. `Accept` refuses lower epochs, so the fence lives in the storage path, not in the leader's process. `TestOldLeaderFencedAtStorage` shows that a former leader's write is rejected even though its own code is unchanged.

**Asynchronous consumer with a bounded loss window.** The analytics node is excluded from the quorum, and it advances only through `Ship`. `Lag` makes the loss window measurable, and `Append` uses it to gate new work. `TestAsyncGateBoundsLoss` shows that exactly three writes are accepted before the gate closes at a bound of two entries. The loss from a consumer failure is therefore capped by configuration, not by luck.

**Leaderless quorum with overlap.** `Put` succeeds on W acknowledgments and `Get` consults R nodes. With W = R = 3 and N = 5, every triple of readers includes a node that acknowledged the write. The exhaustive test checks all ten reader sets, and it rebuilds stale nodes for each case, so read repair cannot mask a broken overlap. `NewQuorumStore` refuses sloppy quorums for decision data and refuses quorum sizes that do not overlap.

**Multi-leader version vectors.** `Reconcile` returns the newer contact when one clock descends from the other. When neither does, it keeps both values as siblings. The concurrent address edits from the worked example survive as siblings, and a later edit that has seen both resolves them.

## What the Model Leaves Out

The model is deliberately incomplete, and a production system needs the missing parts. It has no network, so partitions are expressed only by the `up` flag, and a former leader that can reach some nodes but not others is not modeled. Truncation is applied without checking commit indexes, which a real protocol must do. Elections are not timed, so the choice of candidate is made by the test. Sloppy quorums are refused but not implemented, and anti-entropy with Merkle trees is not included. Each of these needs its own design and its own failure tests, and each is covered in the earlier sections.

Key takeaway: Each replication strategy reduces to a small set of checkable rules in code, which are commit only at a majority, refuse stale candidates and stale epochs at storage, gate asynchronous work by a lag bound, require read and write sets to overlap, and keep concurrent multi-leader edits as siblings, and for Kestrel a test for each rule is what turns a strategy choice into a verified guarantee.

# Core Concepts in Sharding and Partitioning

The overview established that partitioning gives each slice of the dataset its own owner, which removes the throughput and size ceiling of a single consensus group. The hard part is not the division itself. It is the set of decisions that follow: which key to divide by, how to map keys to owners, how clients find the owner, how ownership moves, and how operations that cross slices behave. This section covers five concepts that determine whether a partitioned design holds up. Kestrel, our payments ledger on 5 nodes across `us-east`, `eu-west`, and `ap-south`, anchors each one.

## 1. The Partition Key Determines Skew, and Skew Sets the Ceiling

The partition key decides which records are grouped together. Two properties matter more than any others: how evenly the key spreads load, and how often an operation touches more than one key. Hash partitioning spreads distinct keys evenly, but it cannot spread a single key. Every write for a key lands on the partition that owns it, regardless of how many partitions exist.

For Kestrel, accounts are the natural key, because most operations touch one account. The limit appears with a merchant that receives a large share of payments. Suppose the platform sustains 3,000 payments per second at peak and one merchant account receives 40 percent of them. That is 1,200 writes per second to a single account, all owned by one partition and sequenced by one leader. If a single group can sustain only a fraction of that commit rate on its critical path, adding partitions does nothing for that merchant. The ceiling is set by the hottest key, not by the average.

The standard remedy changes the data model. A hot balance can be split into sub-balances, and each debit draws from one of them. The difficulty is the balance floor. A single balance can be checked against its own value, but ten sub-balances cannot each check the total. The working solution is to allocate a fixed quota to each sub-balance, so each enforces its own floor, and to rebalance quotas through a coordinated transfer when one runs low. The total never goes negative, because the quotas sum to at most the balance. The cost is that a merchant with a nearly empty sub-balance may be refused while another sub-balance holds unused quota, so the quota policy is a business rule, not only an engineering choice.

Skew must be measured before it is designed around. Track write rate per key, not per partition, and alert when the top keys carry a fixed share of load. A partition that looks balanced in aggregate can hide one key that will saturate it.

## 2. Mapping Schemes Trade Ordering Against Balance and Movement Cost

Once a key is chosen, a scheme maps keys to partitions. There are three common schemes, and each one fixes a different trade-off.

**Range partitioning** assigns contiguous key ranges to partitions. It keeps keys in order, which makes range scans cheap, such as all payments for one account in a date window if the key includes a timestamp. Its weakness is skew: sequential keys, such as identifiers generated from a clock, all land on the newest range and overload one partition.

**Hash partitioning** applies a hash to the key before mapping it to a partition. It spreads sequential and clustered keys evenly, at the cost of destroying order, so range queries must visit every partition.

**Directory partitioning** stores an explicit map from key to partition. It can move any individual key and can place related keys together, but the directory itself is state that must be replicated, cached, and kept consistent.

The naive hash scheme has a serious movement problem. If a ledger uses `hash(account) mod n` and grows from 5 partitions to 6, most accounts change partition, and every one of them must be moved. Consistent hashing addresses this. Each partition is placed at several points on a ring, and a key belongs to the next point clockwise. Adding a partition takes ownership of only the ranges adjacent to its points.

```go
package routing

import (
	"hash/fnv"
	"sort"
)

// Ring places each partition at several virtual points so that adding one
// partition moves only the keys adjacent to its points, and load stays even.
type Ring struct {
	points []uint32
	owner  map[uint32]string
}

func NewRing(partitions []string, vnodes int) *Ring {
	r := &Ring{owner: make(map[uint32]string)}
	for _, p := range partitions {
		for v := 0; v < vnodes; v++ {
			h := hash32(p + "#" + itoa(v))
			r.points = append(r.points, h)
			r.owner[h] = p
		}
	}
	sort.Slice(r.points, func(i, j int) bool { return r.points[i] < r.points[j] })
	return r
}

// Lookup returns the partition that owns key.
func (r *Ring) Lookup(key string) string {
	h := hash32(key)
	i := sort.Search(len(r.points), func(i int) bool { return r.points[i] >= h })
	if i == len(r.points) {
		i = 0 // wrap around the ring
	}
	return r.owner[r.points[i]]
}

func hash32(s string) uint32 {
	f := fnv.New32a()
	f.Write([]byte(s))
	return f.Sum32()
}

func itoa(v int) string {
	if v == 0 {
		return "0"
	}
	var b []byte
	for v > 0 {
		b = append([]byte{byte('0' + v%10)}, b...)
		v /= 10
	}
	return string(b)
}
```

For Kestrel, the choice is between directory and consistent hashing. A directory lets operations move a specific merchant to a dedicated partition with a known placement, which suits a small number of large accounts. Consistent hashing suits the long tail of accounts, where movement should be automatic and uniform. Many production layouts combine them: a ring for ordinary accounts and a directory overlay for the handful of accounts that need explicit placement.

## 3. Routing Metadata Must Carry an Ownership Epoch

Clients must know which partition owns a key, and they usually learn this from a cached routing table. Any cache can be stale. After a rebalance, a client may send a debit for an account to the former owner, which may still accept it if nothing stops it.

The defense is an ownership epoch. Each partition records the epoch at which it acquired each range, and every request carries the epoch under which the client routed it. A partition accepts a request only when the client's epoch matches its own, and it returns a retryable error otherwise, prompting the client to refresh its map.

```go
package partition

import (
	"errors"
	"sync"
)

var (
	ErrStaleRouting = errors.New("routing epoch is stale: refresh the map")
	ErrNotOwner     = errors.New("partition does not own this account")
)

// Partition accepts writes only for accounts it owns, and only when the
// caller routed the request under the partition's current epoch.
type Partition struct {
	mu       sync.Mutex
	epoch    uint64
	owned    map[string]bool
	balances map[string]int64
}

func (p *Partition) Debit(routeEpoch uint64, acct string, cents int64) error {
	p.mu.Lock()
	defer p.mu.Unlock()

	if routeEpoch != p.epoch {
		return ErrStaleRouting
	}
	if !p.owned[acct] {
		return ErrNotOwner
	}
	if p.balances[acct] < cents {
		return ErrInsufficientFunds
	}
	p.balances[acct] -= cents
	return nil
}
```

The check must be enforced at the point of commit, as with fencing in the replication material. A check at the gateway can be bypassed by a client holding an old connection, and a check on a partition that has just lost ownership must still refuse. Routing metadata is therefore not a hint. It is an authority that the owning partition verifies on every write.

Routing caches should also expire on a schedule, and clients should treat a rejection as a signal to refresh, not as a failure to retry blindly. Blind retries under a stale map can hammer the former owner and delay the client's discovery of the new one.

## 4. Rebalancing Must Never Produce Two Owners

Moving a range of accounts from one partition to another is the most failure-prone operation in a partitioned system. The invariant to protect is that at every moment, at most one partition accepts writes for each account, and every write accepted by the old owner is visible to the new owner before ownership moves.

A safe handoff proceeds in stages. The source partition freezes writes for the moving range, so no new debits are accepted there. It then transfers the committed state and its log position for the range to the destination. The destination catches up to the frozen position and records the new epoch. Finally, the routing metadata is updated, and the source marks the range as no longer owned. Each step has a failure mode: a crash during the freeze leaves the range unavailable until recovery completes, and a crash after the transfer but before the metadata update leaves two partitions with copies of the range, one of which must be designated authoritative by epoch.

The freeze is a deliberate availability cost. For Kestrel, moving 1,000 accounts from one group to another might block writes to those accounts for the duration of the transfer. The rebalance should be sized so that the freeze is short, and it should run in a low-traffic window with the freeze duration measured and recorded. A rebalance that blocks a merchant for thirty seconds at peak is an incident, even if it is correct.

The most common mistake is to skip the freeze and rely on epochs alone. Epochs prevent a stale client from writing to the old owner after the handoff, but they do not stop writes that the old owner accepted before the handoff and that the destination has not yet seen. The freeze, or an equivalent barrier that confirms the destination has caught up, is what closes that gap.

## 5. Cross-Partition Operations Give Up Global Order

Each partition runs its own consensus group and offers linearizability for its own keys. Across partitions, no global order exists unless the system builds one. Two operations on different partitions can be observed in different orders by different readers, and that is correct behavior, not a defect.

This matters in two places. The first is multi-account operations such as transfers, which need an atomic commitment protocol across the two groups. The next section covers that protocol. Without it, a transfer can be half-applied, as shown in the replication closing section.

The second is reads that span partitions. A report of total payment volume per merchant queries every partition, and each partition answers at its own point in time. A total computed this way may include a payment on one partition and omit a related refund on another, so the report reflects no single moment that ever existed. Two options exist. The first is a coordinated snapshot, in which every partition returns its state at the same log position, which requires the partitions to agree on a common timestamp or index. The second is to accept the skew and label the report with the per-partition positions it used. For financial reporting, the second is common, provided the labels are published with the number, so that an auditor can reconstruct what the report saw.

The scatter-gather pattern should also be bounded. A query that touches every partition is only as fast as the slowest one, and a single partition in a degraded region can stall the whole report. Timeouts, partial results with a completeness marker, and projections built from the committed logs of each partition are the usual defenses, and the choice should be made per query, not once for the platform.

## Connecting the Concepts

These five concepts interact. The partition key determines skew and therefore how much load a scheme must spread. The scheme determines how much data moves on a rebalance. The routing epoch determines whether a stale client can write during and after a move. The handoff protocol determines whether ownership is ever doubled. Cross-partition behavior determines which guarantees a client can rely on once the dataset is no longer in one place.

A design review for a partitioned dataset should be able to state the answer to each: what the key is and what the hottest key's share of load is, which scheme is used and how much data moves when a partition is added, how a client with a stale map is refused, what the freeze duration is during a rebalance, and which operations cross partitions and what they guarantee.

Key takeaway: Sharding works when the partition key spreads load and keeps common operations local, the mapping scheme limits how much data moves on change, routing carries an ownership epoch that every owner verifies, rebalancing freezes and hands off state so that two owners never accept writes for the same account, and cross-partition operations are designed explicitly because no global order exists by default. For Kestrel, that means accounts as the key, sub-balance quotas for hot merchants, consistent hashing with a directory overlay, epoch-checked writes, and transfers whose cross-partition atomicity is handled before the first split goes live.

# A Sharding and Partitioning Model in Go

This section builds a small, deterministic model of a partitioned ledger for Kestrel. It has a consistent-hash ring with virtual points, a directory overlay for pinned accounts, a routing map carrying epochs, partitions that enforce ownership and epochs at commit time, a handoff sequence that moves accounts between partitions, and tests for the properties the earlier sections required. The model has no network and no consensus. Each partition is a single in-memory owner, so the code isolates the partitioning logic. Consensus within each partition is covered in the earlier sections.

The code is one package, `partition`, split across two files.

## Routing: The Ring, the Directory, and the Map

```go
package partition

import (
	"hash/fnv"
	"sort"
	"strconv"
	"sync"
)

func hash64(s string) uint64 {
	h := fnv.New64a()
	h.Write([]byte(s))
	return h.Sum64()
}

// Ring maps keys to partitions. Each partition holds several virtual points,
// so adding a partition takes over only the keys that fall next to its points.
type Ring struct {
	points []uint64
	owner  map[uint64]string
}

func NewRing(partitions []string, vnodes int) *Ring {
	r := &Ring{owner: make(map[uint64]string)}
	for _, p := range partitions {
		for v := 0; v < vnodes; v++ {
			h := hash64(p + "#" + strconv.Itoa(v))
			r.points = append(r.points, h)
			r.owner[h] = p
		}
	}
	sort.Slice(r.points, func(i, j int) bool { return r.points[i] < r.points[j] })
	return r
}

// Lookup returns the partition that owns key: the first point clockwise.
func (r *Ring) Lookup(key string) string {
	h := hash64(key)
	i := sort.Search(len(r.points), func(i int) bool { return r.points[i] >= h })
	if i == len(r.points) {
		i = 0
	}
	return r.owner[r.points[i]]
}

// Route is what a client receives: where to send a request, and the epoch
// of the owner at the time the map was published.
type Route struct {
	Partition string
	Epoch     uint64
}

// Map is an immutable routing snapshot. Overrides pin specific accounts,
// such as large merchants, ahead of the ring.
type Map struct {
	Version   uint64
	ring      *Ring
	overrides map[string]string
	epochs    map[string]uint64
}

func (m *Map) Route(acct string) Route {
	p := m.ring.Lookup(acct)
	if o, ok := m.overrides[acct]; ok {
		p = o
	}
	return Route{Partition: p, Epoch: m.epochs[p]}
}
```

## Partitions: Ownership and Epochs Enforced at Commit

```go
var (
	ErrStaleRouting = errors.New("routing epoch is stale: refresh the map")
	ErrNotOwner     = errors.New("partition does not own this account")
	ErrFrozen       = errors.New("account is frozen for handoff")
	ErrDoubleOwner  = errors.New("account already owned by this partition")
	ErrInsufficient = errors.New("insufficient funds")
)

// Partition owns the accounts present in its balances map. It refuses any
// request whose routing epoch differs from its own, and any request for a
// frozen or unowned account, at the point where the write is applied.
type Partition struct {
	mu       sync.Mutex
	id       string
	epoch    uint64
	applied  uint64
	balances map[string]int64
	frozen   map[string]bool
}

func NewPartition(id string) *Partition {
	return &Partition{
		id:       id,
		epoch:    1,
		balances: make(map[string]int64),
		frozen:   make(map[string]bool),
	}
}

func (p *Partition) Epoch() uint64 {
	p.mu.Lock()
	defer p.mu.Unlock()
	return p.epoch
}

func (p *Partition) Applied() uint64 {
	p.mu.Lock()
	defer p.mu.Unlock()
	return p.applied
}

// Open seeds an account this partition owns. It is used for setup only.
func (p *Partition) Open(acct string, cents int64) {
	p.mu.Lock()
	defer p.mu.Unlock()
	p.balances[acct] = cents
}

// Debit applies a debit if the caller routed under the current epoch, the
// account is owned here, it is not frozen, and the balance covers it.
func (p *Partition) Debit(rt Route, acct string, cents int64) (uint64, error) {
	p.mu.Lock()
	defer p.mu.Unlock()

	if rt.Epoch != p.epoch {
		return 0, ErrStaleRouting
	}
	bal, owned := p.balances[acct]
	if !owned {
		return 0, ErrNotOwner
	}
	if p.frozen[acct] {
		return 0, ErrFrozen
	}
	if bal < cents {
		return 0, ErrInsufficient
	}
	p.balances[acct] = bal - cents
	p.applied++
	return p.applied, nil
}

// Freeze refuses further writes to accts. It fails if any is not owned here.
func (p *Partition) Freeze(accts []string) error {
	p.mu.Lock()
	defer p.mu.Unlock()
	for _, a := range accts {
		if _, ok := p.balances[a]; !ok {
			return ErrNotOwner
		}
	}
	for _, a := range accts {
		p.frozen[a] = true
	}
	return nil
}

func (p *Partition) Unfreeze(accts []string) {
	p.mu.Lock()
	defer p.mu.Unlock()
	for _, a := range accts {
		delete(p.frozen, a)
	}
}

// Snapshot returns the balances of accts as they stand at this partition.
func (p *Partition) Snapshot(accts []string) map[string]int64 {
	p.mu.Lock()
	defer p.mu.Unlock()
	out := make(map[string]int64, len(accts))
	for _, a := range accts {
		if v, ok := p.balances[a]; ok {
			out[a] = v
		}
	}
	return out
}

// Install takes ownership of a snapshot and moves the partition to epoch.
// It refuses any account the partition already holds.
func (p *Partition) Install(snap map[string]int64, epoch uint64) error {
	p.mu.Lock()
	defer p.mu.Unlock()
	for a := range snap {
		if _, dup := p.balances[a]; dup {
			return ErrDoubleOwner
		}
	}
	for a, v := range snap {
		p.balances[a] = v
	}
	p.epoch = epoch
	return nil
}

// Release gives up accts and advances the epoch, so any client still routing
// here under the old epoch is refused and must refresh.
func (p *Partition) Release(accts []string) {
	p.mu.Lock()
	defer p.mu.Unlock()
	for _, a := range accts {
		delete(p.balances, a)
		delete(p.frozen, a)
	}
	p.epoch++
}

// Sum returns the total balance this partition currently owns.
func (p *Partition) Sum() int64 {
	p.mu.Lock()
	defer p.mu.Unlock()
	var total int64
	for _, v := range p.balances {
		total += v
	}
	return total
}
```

## The Cluster: Publishing Maps and Moving Accounts

```go
// Cluster ties the partitions to the current routing map and performs
// handoffs between them.
type Cluster struct {
	mu     sync.Mutex
	parts  map[string]*Partition
	routes *Map
}

func NewCluster(ids []string, vnodes int) *Cluster {
	c := &Cluster{parts: make(map[string]*Partition)}
	for _, id := range ids {
		c.parts[id] = NewPartition(id)
	}
	m := &Map{
		Version:   1,
		ring:      NewRing(ids, vnodes),
		overrides: make(map[string]string),
		epochs:    make(map[string]uint64),
	}
	for id, p := range c.parts {
		m.epochs[id] = p.Epoch()
	}
	c.routes = m
	return c
}

func (c *Cluster) Route(acct string) Route {
	c.mu.Lock()
	defer c.mu.Unlock()
	return c.routes.Route(acct)
}

// Open creates an account at the partition the current map assigns it.
func (c *Cluster) Open(acct string, cents int64) {
	c.parts[c.Route(acct).Partition].Open(acct, cents)
}

// Handoff moves accts from src to dst. The order matters. Freezing first stops
// new writes at the source. Installing at the destination before the source
// releases means no balance is ever missing. The map is published last, after
// both partitions hold their final epochs, so clients are redirected only once
// the destination is ready. A failure before Release leaves the source owner
// and unfrozen.
func (c *Cluster) Handoff(src, dst string, accts []string) error {
	s, d := c.parts[src], c.parts[dst]
	if err := s.Freeze(accts); err != nil {
		return err
	}
	snap := s.Snapshot(accts)
	if err := d.Install(snap, d.Epoch()+1); err != nil {
		s.Unfreeze(accts)
		return err
	}
	s.Release(accts)
	c.publish(accts, dst)
	return nil
}

// publish builds the next map: moved accounts are pinned to dst, and the
// epochs are captured after both partitions have changed.
func (c *Cluster) publish(moved []string, dst string) {
	c.mu.Lock()
	defer c.mu.Unlock()

	next := &Map{
		Version:   c.routes.Version + 1,
		ring:      c.routes.ring,
		overrides: make(map[string]string),
		epochs:    make(map[string]uint64),
	}
	for a, p := range c.routes.overrides {
		next.overrides[a] = p
	}
	for _, a := range moved {
		next.overrides[a] = dst
	}
	for id, p := range c.parts {
		next.epochs[id] = p.Epoch()
	}
	c.routes = next
}

// Debit routes with the current map and applies the debit at the owner.
func (c *Cluster) Debit(acct string, cents int64) error {
	rt := c.Route(acct)
	_, err := c.parts[rt.Partition].Debit(rt, acct, cents)
	return err
}

// Total sums every partition's balances and reports each partition's
// applied position. Summing before and after a handoff checks conservation.
func (c *Cluster) Total() (int64, map[string]uint64) {
	var sum int64
	pos := make(map[string]uint64, len(c.parts))
	for id, p := range c.parts {
		sum += p.Sum()
		pos[id] = p.Applied()
	}
	return sum, pos
}
```

